Kept the printing logic direct. For row `row`, the number `row` is printed exactly `row` times. A newline is added after each row.

For `n = 4`, the rows are `1`, `22`, `333`, and `4444`.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern4(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int column = 1; column <= row; column++) {
                System.out.print(row);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the total number of printed values is `1 + 2 + ... + n`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output itself.

The pattern can be made with one outer loop and a repeated text value. For each row, `str(row) * row` creates the row without needing a second loop.

For example, when `n = 4`, the fourth iteration creates `"4444"`. If `n` is zero or negative, the loop does not run, so there is no unwanted output.

```python
class Solution:
    def pattern4(self, n):
        for row in range(1, n + 1):
            print(str(row) * row)
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because Python creates strings whose lengths are `1` through `n`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the largest row string.

This version creates one row as a string before printing it. The inner loop adds the current row number, so it also works when `n` is greater than 9 because it stores the full number as text.

The input is not changed. The edge case `n = 0` simply prints nothing.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern4(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            std::string currentRow;
            std::string value = std::to_string(row);

            for (int column = 1; column <= row; column++) {
                currentRow += value;
            }

            std::cout << currentRow << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for creating all row strings.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the largest temporary row.

Instead of printing every character immediately, this version stores the complete pattern in a `StringBuilder` and prints it once. Each row adds its number repeatedly, followed by a newline.

A dry run for `n = 3` builds `1\n22\n333\n` in that order. This is useful when many small print calls should be avoided.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern4(int n) {
        StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder();

        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int count = 0; count < row; count++) {
                output.append(row);
            }
            output.append('\n');
        }

        System.out.print(output.toString());
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every pattern value is appended once.

Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for storing the complete output before printing.

